comments.body:
@reporter If you are talking about the latency of insert request, this could be expected.  @participant2 could you please help to take a look?

/assign @participant2 
/unassign

@participant2 out of curiosity, what is different about woodpecker compared to the other MQs that causes the slow individual inserts? 

I am going to benchmark the batch insert time as well. In that case, would woodpecker work better? What MQ would you recommend for individual inserts? 


For a bit more context, I am a PhD student who is working to benchmark the major distributed vector databases on HPC systems. Any insight you can provide would be greatly appreciated 😀

@participant2 
 there should be some metrics to change the 200ms merge latency? for people who use S3 this can be tuned to 10 ms.

@reporter 

if you can test batch or concurrent  insert it would be helpful.

@participant3 I will try it. I will also try not using the memory buffer and instead the Quorum buffer. 

@participant2 @participant3 
Follow up questions. Any suggestions would be great. 

1. Is there a way to time from the first insert to when all inserted data becomes searchable in Milvus? My understanding is that the client insert function returns when the data is received by the WAL. There is then some gap (however small) between when the streaming node reads from the WAL and inserts it into a growing segment. I want to be able to measure how long before all inserted data is searchable.

2. In a perfect world, I would also like to be able to measure insert speed when every write is immediately searchable. My understanding is that changing the consistency level will have queries wait for a given timestamp to be processed, but it won't make the writes themselves immediately searchable.
